---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ISSUES, NEW_ISSUE, VERCEL_PRIVACY, GITHUB_PRIVACY, CLARITY_PRIVACY } from '../lib/links';
const updated = '17 November 2026';
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="The Shotlight app collects nothing: no account, no analytics, no network code. What the app and this website do with your data.">
  <div class="wrap page">
    <header class="head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h1>The app collects nothing.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Short version: the app never goes online and never sends anything anywhere. This website counts visits, to see which parts people find useful. Here are the details.</p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The app</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No account, no analytics, no crash reports, no update checks.</strong> Shotlight has no networking code at all. Every release is checked for network APIs before it’s published, and you can <a href="/verify">check it yourself</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Your data stays on your Mac.</strong> Your screenshots, the text read from them and the search index stay in <code>~/Library/Application Support/Shotlight</code>. Settings are stored in the usual macOS preferences file.</li>
        <li><strong>Naming happens on-device.</strong> Shotlight reads screenshots with Apple Vision and, where available, Apple Intelligence. Both run on your Mac. Nothing is uploaded, and there’s no model to download.</li>
        <li><strong>Secrets stay out.</strong> When a screenshot contains something like a password, API key, card number or one-time code, Shotlight keeps it out of the file name and the search index.</li>
        <li><strong>Only the folders you pick.</strong> Shotlight reads your screenshot folder and any folders you add, and nothing else. It doesn’t need Screen Recording or Accessibility permission.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>To remove everything Shotlight stored, quit it and delete <code>~/Library/Application Support/Shotlight</code>.</p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Visit analytics.</strong> This site uses <a href={CLARITY_PRIVACY}>Microsoft Clarity</a> to see how many people visit, where they came from, and which pages and buttons they use, such as the Download button. Clarity records clicks, scrolling and mouse movement on these pages, and may set cookies to tell a returning visitor from a new one. It isn’t used for ads, and it sees nothing from the app or your Mac. Content blockers stop it, and the site works the same without it.</li>
        <li><strong>The like button.</strong> To count each visitor once, your browser keeps a random ID in local storage, and the site stores the IDs of people who liked it. The ID isn’t linked to you or to Clarity. To stop the button being abused, the site briefly keeps a scrambled (hashed) form of your IP address, never the address itself, and deletes it after an hour. The count is stored with Upstash, through Vercel.</li>
        <li><strong>Everything else is served from this site.</strong> No other third-party scripts, fonts or embeds.</li>
        <li><strong>Hosting.</strong> The site is hosted on Vercel. Like any web host, Vercel may keep server logs (such as IP addresses) for security and operations. See <a href={VERCEL_PRIVACY}>Vercel’s privacy policy</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Downloads.</strong> The app is downloaded from GitHub Releases, and bug reports go to GitHub Issues, so GitHub’s <a href={GITHUB_PRIVACY}>privacy statement</a> applies there.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Questions about privacy? <a href={NEW_ISSUE}>Open an issue</a> on GitHub, or browse <a href={ISSUES}>existing ones</a>. Issues are public, so don’t include anything personal.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page { max-width: 820px; padding-top: 80px; padding-bottom: 96px; }
  .head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 56px); line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -0.04em; font-weight: 600; }
  .updated { font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-3); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .page { padding-top: 48px; padding-bottom: 72px; } }
</style>
